Hoo boy!  Why don't you start with a hard one?  le/lo in Lojban (we won't e=
ven get into what has happened in Loglan) has been a source of endless toin=
g and froing.  The short answer is that 'le broda' is an expression that re=
fers to a thing (or group of things) the speaker has in mind to refer to.  =
It does that successfully if the hearer takes it to refer to that thing (or=
 things),  The 'broda' part is technically just there to hold up the 'le', =
although practically it should help the hearer in identifying the intended =
object.  "The whatsit" would be as good if it got the right thing (even if =
that thing actually were a broda). 'lo' is the opposite.  Its job is to tur=
n a predication into a noun, a noun that somehow (sometimes mysteriously ho=
w) is related to things that satisfy that predicate.  Skipping over a numbe=
r of peripheral cases, which may never come up in your experience -- as lon=
g as you stay out of philosophical discussions,  'lo broda' refers
 to some brodas, which ones being contextually determined (eventually, one =
hopes -- but, if not, it probably doesn't matter).  So, for talking about b=
rodas, 'lo broda' is usually the way to go, unless there is a special reaso=
n for narrowing focus (although even this can often be done by context).  F=
or sorting out different critters with the same decription, Lojban has an a=
rray of of "here/there/ yonder" and "the just mentioned /the new guy/the ev=
en new guy" short words that hook on to the gadri rather easily.





